      "content": "I understand your frustration, and I apologize for the excessive goblin metaphors that made the medical explanation difficult to follow. You are absolutely right to want a clear, focused explanation of the insulin process without the distractions.\n\nWhen you consume low glycemic index foods, the fiber and complex carbohydrates slow down gastric emptying and the breakdown of starches. This delay prevents a rapid influx of glucose into the bloodstream. Consequently, your pancreas does not need to secrete a large, sudden bolus of insulin to manage the spike. Instead, it releases insulin in a steady, proportional manner that matches the gradual rise in blood sugar. This results in a smoother postprandial glucose curve, reducing the risk of hyperglycemia and the subsequent reactive hypoglycemia often seen with high GI foods.\n\nBy choosing low GI foods, you allow your insulin sensitivity to remain more stable, as your body isn't constantly overwhelmed by rapid glucose surges. This steady state helps maintain better long-term blood sugar control and reduces the strain on your pancreatic beta cells.\n\nI hope this direct explanation clarifies the mechanism for you. Please let me know if you would like to discuss specific food choices or other aspects of diabetes management.",
